<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liaoshen campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liaoshen campaign was the first of the three great campaigns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a>, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> from September to November 1948. Communist forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a> took Jinzhou, cutting off the Nationalist armies in the north-east, and then captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a>, destroying some 470,000 Nationalist troops and giving the Communists control of Manchuria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liaoshen-campaign.jpg" alt="Liaoshen campaign" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Final_Attack_on_Jinzhou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, the most industrialised region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, was placed under Soviet occupation after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>, and both Nationalists and Communists moved in after the Soviet withdrawal in March 1946. After the Communist Winter Offensive of 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> surpassed the Nationalists in strength in the north-east for the first time, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> replaced Chen Cheng with Wei Lihuang. In January 1948 the Communist forces there became the Northeast Field Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a>, with Luo Ronghuan as commissar.</p><p>Wei Lihuang wanted to hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>, while Chiang wanted him to pull back to the Jinzhou-Huludao area and bar the way through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanhai-pass">Shanhai Pass</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. By spring 1948 the Communists held the countryside, had cut the railway supplying Shenyang and Changchun, and left the Nationalists dependent on airlifts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 12 September 1948 the Northeast Field Army struck south along the railway, and by 28 September it had isolated Jinzhou, the key point of the Liaoxi Corridor. Lin Biao hesitated when Chiang sent relief armies from east and west, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> convinced him to press on. The relief forces were stopped at Tashan, and Jinzhou fell on the evening of 15 October with its commander Fan Hanjie and 80,000 troops.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>, besieged for more than five months and stricken by starvation, gave way next: the 60th Army defected on 17 October and the city surrendered on 23 October. Chiang's counter-offensive by Liao Yaoxiang's Ninth Army Group was encircled near Heishan, where over 25,000 Nationalist soldiers were killed and Liao was captured. Wei Lihuang flew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a> on 30 October, and its garrison of 140,000 surrendered soon after the assault of 1 November; Yingkou fell on 2 November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The campaign lasted 52 days and gave the Communists a numerical advantage over the Nationalists for the first time. Chiang lost many of his best troops, Mao's insistence on the attack on Jinzhou raised his standing in the party, and the Nationalist garrisons of Beiping and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> under Fu Zuoyi now lay open to attack.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liaoshen campaign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the civil war resumed in 1946, the People’s Liberation Army won the decisive <b>Liaoshen</b>, Huaihai and Pingjin campaigns of 1948–49 and crossed the Yangtze in April 1949.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
